-- title:
--   Cartier quadruple of the edge family at a node point
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota \colon \mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to \mathbb{W}(k)$ a ring homomorphism, where `Zp2 p` denotes the Witt vectors of the field `GaloisField p 2`. Write $W = \mathbb{W}(k)$, $\bar W = W/pW$ (the quotient by `pIdeal p W`, the ideal generated by $p$), and $\bar\jmath$ for the induced structure map $\mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to \bar W$, the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map. A `FormalODModule p B` consists of a $2$-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $B$, an action `act` of $\mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ by endomorphisms of $F$ (additive and multiplicative in the parameter, with `act 1` the identity) and a further endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Witt Frobenius $\sigma$.
--
--   *Data and hypotheses on $\Phi$.* $\Phi$ is such a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $\bar W$, subject to: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. the submodules $\mathrm{Lie}^0 = \bigcap_a \ker(\mathrm{lieAct}(a) - \bar\jmath(a)\,\mathrm{id})$ and $\mathrm{Lie}^1$ of the Lie module are complementary and each invertible; `hΦ4`, that $\mathrm{act}(p)$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that $\mathrm{Lie}^0$ is contained in the kernel of `Φ.lieVarpi`, the linear part of $\varpi$ acting on the Lie module; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces of degrees $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ are complementary subgroups, where the piece of degree $n$ consists of those $f$ with $\mathrm{endAct}(\mathrm{act}(\tau c))f = \mathrm{homothety}(\bar\jmath(\tau c)^{p^n})f$ for all $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$, $\tau$ denoting the Teichmüller lift. These data produce the graded Cartier module datum $D_\Phi =$ `Φ.toGradedCartierModuleData` $\bar\jmath$ `hcΦ`, whose underlying module is the Cartier module of $\Phi$, with Frobenius, the integral Verschiebung, the operator induced by $\varpi$, and the two graded pieces. Further, $r_\Phi \colon (\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathbb{Z}_p) \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$ is an additive map into the $N$-module of $D_\Phi$, and the hypothesis `hrΦ` requires that for every additive map $L$ from $D_\Phi.M$ to $D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$ which is a canonical $L$-map (a Cartier $L$-map admitting a lift along a surjection from a special graded Cartier module over a $p$-torsion-free base), $r_\Phi$ maps $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto `etaPiece L _ 0`, the intersection of the $\eta$-subgroup of $L$ with the degree-$0$ piece of the $N$-module.
--
--   *The matrix.* $g$ is an element of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ whose matrix is, by `hg`, the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$.
--
--   *The edge family.* $E =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar W$ is the characteristic-$p$ edge chart ring `FormalOmega.chartERing` $\bar W\,0\,p$, with its two distinguished elements $\xi$ and $\eta$. $X$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $E$ and $\gamma_0,\gamma_1$ are elements of its Cartier module such that: `hγ`, $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $E \leftarrow \bar W \leftarrow W$ composed with $\iota$, that is $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of degree $i$ and the determinant of the matrix of tangent vectors $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_l)$ is a unit; `hγa`, $X$ has structure constants $\gamma$ with the edge constants `edgeConstants p ξ η`, meaning that for all $i$ and all $N$ the element $\varpi(\gamma_i)$ equals $\sum_{m<N} V^m(\mathrm{homothety}(a_{m,i})\gamma_{\mathrm{piIndex}(m,i)})$ plus $V^N$ of some element; `hXs`, $X$ is special for that structure map; and `hX4`, $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   *The node point.* $f_0 \colon E \to \bar W$ is a ring homomorphism which by `hf₀` splits the structure map $\bar W \to E$ and which by `hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η` kills both $\xi$ and $\eta$.
--
--   *The isogeny over the node and its rigidification.* $m$ is a natural number and $\rho_0$ a $2$-tuple of power series over $\bar W$ which by `hρ₀` is an isogeny of height $4m$ from $\Phi$ to $X \otimes_{f_0} \bar W$, i.e. an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Let $t_0$ be the rigidified datum over $\bar W$ with underlying module $X \otimes_{f_0}\bar W$, level $m$ and series the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. The hypotheses on $t_0$ are: `hOD₀`, that the series of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $t_0.\overline{\Phi}$ to $t_0.\overline{X}$; `hcb`, that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of the Cartier module of $t_0.\overline{X}$ pushed along the identity of $\bar W$ are complementary, for the structure map `jSbar`; and `hcΦg`, the corresponding complementarity for $\Phi$ pushed along the identity of $\bar W$. Finally `hN` is a normalisation of the rigidification numerator: there is $a \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for both $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$,
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota, \mathrm{hc}\Phi, r_\Phi, t_0, \mathrm{hOD}_0, \mathrm{id}_{\bar W}, \mathrm{hcb}, \mathrm{hc}\Phi g)(e_i) = p^{a+m}\cdot v_i ,$$
--   where $\mathrm{rigidNum}$ is the composite of $r_\Phi$ with the two $N$-module maps induced by base change of $\Phi$ and by the series of $t_0$, $e_i$ is the $i$-th standard vector of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, and $v_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\gamma_0', 0)$, $v_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}(\mathrm{nMk}(\gamma_1', 0))$, with $\gamma_i'$ the successive base changes of $\gamma_i$ along $f_0$, along the quotient of $\bar W$ by $p$, and along the reduction of the identity of $\bar W$.
--
--   *The explicit edge isogeny.* $\rho_1$ is a morphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules over $E$ from $(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar W)\otimes_{\bar W} E$ to $X$ whose induced map of Cartier modules satisfies, by `hρ₁0` and `hρ₁1`,
--   $$\rho_1(\gamma_0) = p\,\gamma_0 - V\bigl(\tau(\eta^{p^2})\,\gamma_1\bigr), \qquad \rho_1(\gamma_1) = p\,\gamma_1 - V\bigl(\tau(\xi^{p^2})\,\gamma_0\bigr),$$
--   the arguments on the left being the base changes of $\gamma_0,\gamma_1$ along $f_0$ and then along $\bar W \to E$, $V$ the integral Verschiebung and $\tau$ the Teichmüller lift; and by `hρ₁h` the series of $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   *The geometric point.* $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, and $y \colon E \to \Omega$ is a ring homomorphism with $y(\xi) = 0$ and $y(\eta) = 0$ (hypotheses `hyξ`, `hyη`): a point lying on the node of the edge chart.
--
--   *Conclusion.* Let $t$ be the rigidified datum over $E$ with underlying module $X$, level $m+1$, and series the composite (in the sense of `Series.comp`) of the reduction of the series of $\rho_1$ modulo $p$ with the image of $\rho_0$ under $\bar W \to E \to E/pE$. Then there exist a Drinfeld datum $Q$ over $\Omega$ for $K = \mathbb{Q}_p$ and uniformiser $p \in \mathbb{Z}_p$, and a Deligne datum $d$ over $\Omega$ for the same data, such that the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, the datum $t$ pushed forward along $y$ is a Cartier quadruple with datum $Q$, relative to $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$ and the structure map $W \to \bar W \to E \to \Omega$: that is, the series of the pushed datum is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from the base change of $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X \otimes_y \Omega$; there are $\Omega$-linear isomorphisms of $Q.T_0$ with $\mathrm{Lie}^0$ and of $Q.T_1$ with $\mathrm{Lie}^1$ of $X\otimes_y\Omega$ carrying $Q.\Pi_0$ and $Q.\Pi_1$ to the operator `lieVarpi`; and, for every prime $x$ of $\Omega$, the lattices $Q.N_0(x)$ and $Q.N_1(x)$ consist exactly of those vectors admitting an $\eta$-section of the corresponding degree over some localisation of $\Omega$ away from $x$ at which the relevant graded complementarity conditions hold and a canonical $L$-map is chosen, together with further clauses (summarised here) matching $Q.u_0$ and $Q.u_1$ with the classes of such $\eta$-sections modulo the $V$-range under the canonical $L$-maps.
--
--   Second, $Q$ is a quadruple of $d$: for every prime $x$ of $\Omega$, the pair $Q.L_0(x)$, $Q.L_1(x)$ is edge-nondegenerate for $d$ at $x$, and the kernels of $Q.u_0(x)$ and $Q.u_1(x)$ are the lines attached by the localised Deligne datum to $Q.L_0(x)$ and $Q.L_1(x)$ respectively.
--
--   Third, at the standard lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^2 \subset \mathbb{Q}_p^2$ the line of $d$ is the $\Omega$-span of the single element $y(\xi)\otimes e_0 + 1 \otimes e_1$, the tensor products being over $\mathbb{Z}_p$ and $e_0,e_1$ the standard basis vectors of the standard full lattice.
--
--   Fourth, at the translated lattice `FullLattice.act g` $\mathbb{Z}_p^2 = p\mathbb{Z}_p \oplus \mathbb{Z}_p$ the line of $d$ is the image, under the canonical isomorphism `actBaseChange Ω g` of the base-changed lattices, of the $\Omega$-span of $1 \otimes e_0 + y(\eta)\otimes e_1$.
--
--   Since $y(\xi) = y(\eta) = 0$, the last two lines are the span of $1\otimes e_1$ and the image of the span of $1 \otimes e_0$.
--
--   This computes the geometric fibre of the rigidified edge family of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules at a point of the edge chart lying on the node, where both coordinates $\xi$ and $\eta$ vanish, and identifies the resulting Drinfeld quadruple with the Deligne datum whose lines at the two adjacent vertices of the Bruhat–Tits tree are the standard ones; it is the node case in the construction of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation morphism. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq_of_edge_isogeny`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq_of_edge_isogeny), which assembles the fibres over all geometric points of the edge chart.
